<p>The five Gulf markets in the table took about 6,205 MT at an average of about USD 23.4 per kg, all within a band of USD 22-24.5. Bangladesh bought about as much as Kuwait but paid less than 40% of the Gulf price per kilo, which points to lower grades, smaller or split capsules, rather than the same cardamom at a discount. A unit value is not a price, but the tight Gulf band says the qahwa trade buys a narrow range of green, size-graded capsules. The UAE sits slightly below Saudi Arabia and Kuwait, consistent with Dubai traders re-exporting a wider mix. Scenario: a Riyadh coffee-house supplier buying 8mm bold for qahwa and a Dubai trader buying AGS for re-export to price-sensitive markets are both "Gulf buyers", and the trader should expect to sit below the UAE average while the 8mm buyer pays above it.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<!-- insight:spec-to-application -->
<h2>How is cardamom graded, and which grade suits which use?</h2>
<p>By capsule size, colour and how well the capsules are filled. India's AGMARK grading rules define the size marks by the sieve a capsule is retained on: Alleppey Green Extra Bold (AGEB) at 7.0 mm, Alleppey Green Bold (AGB) at 6.0 mm and Alleppey Green Superior (AGS) at 5.0 mm, with Coorg marks on larger sieves. The trade's "8mm bold" sits above AGEB. Fill is measured as litre weight, the grams a litre of capsules weighs: the AGMARK Special quality needs at least 435 g/l, moisture no higher than 11% and volatile oil of at least 4.0%, and the Standard quality 385 g/l, 13% and 3.5%.</p>
<p>The figures map to use. A well-filled 435 g/l capsule carries about 13% more seed per litre than a 385 g/l one, which matters to a qahwa blender who measures by volume. Green colour matters most in retail packs, where at least 95% of the capsules in a lot have to match the declared colour group under the same rules. Bulk culinary and grinding buyers can trade colour for price and use AGS or AGB. Specify your size mark, colour group and minimum litre weight, and tell us the end use; that settles most of the price before the auction market does.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<h2>Where is Indian cardamom grown, and when is it picked?</h2>
<p>Kerala's Idukki district, in the Western Ghats, is India's principal cardamom-growing region, along with Karnataka (Kodagu/Chikmagalur) and Tamil Nadu, which share the altitude and rainfall conditions the crop requires. Unlike most spices with a single harvest window, cardamom pods mature at different times on the same plant, so picking runs in staggered rounds from roughly August through February — a longer supply window than most of our other spice lines, even though it's still a genuinely seasonal crop. Much of the crop is sold through the Spices Board's e-auctions, held at Puttady in Idukki and at Bodinayakanur (Bodi) in Tamil Nadu, and those auction prices are the market's reference.</p>

<!-- insight:container-economics -->
<h2>How much cardamom fits in a container, and what is it worth?</h2>
<p>About 8-10 MT in a 20ft and 16-18 MT in a 40ft, because cardamom is light and fills the box by volume. At the 2024 UAE average of about USD 23.07 per kg, a 9 MT 20ft carries roughly USD 208,000 of cardamom; Saudi Arabia's whole 2024 intake of 1,816 MT is about 200 such containers. Because the box fills by volume, a better-filled lot also loads more kilograms: at 435 g/l against 385 g/l, roughly 13% more cardamom goes into the same space.</p>
<p>Freight is a small share of a cargo worth this much, so the container decision turns on capital, insurance and aroma rather than on freight rate. Insure at full CIF value, check that the letter of credit covers the whole value, and weigh a full container against two smaller lots a few months apart. Scenario: a Kuwait importer that turns over about 4-5 MT a quarter buys half a 20ft every quarter instead of a full 40ft once a year, ties up a quarter of the capital, and sells cardamom that is never more than a few months from packing.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<h2>How long does cardamom keep its aroma after packing?</h2>
<p>Cardamom is more sensitive to storage conditions than most spices — its essential oils, which carry the aroma buyers pay for, degrade with heat and light exposure over time. Store in cool, dark, dry conditions and expect noticeably better retained aroma from stock used within 12-18 months of packing versus older stock, even when moisture and other specifications remain technically within range. If your business holds inventory for extended periods, factor this into your ordering cadence — buying smaller, more frequent shipments often beats one large annual order for a product this aroma-sensitive.</p>

